# Manifests

The package groups in `package-groups/` are the openSUSE Tumbleweed patterns of
the same name, carried over as Guix manifest files.  The design follows
openSUSE TW substantially — the group concept, the naming and the package
selections come from it — while the files themselves use the manifest format
the Guix manual defines, so the package manager consumes them directly.

Derivation: the identifier and the summary come from the pattern RPMs
(`patterns-base-20241218`, `patterns-server-20250313`), and the package list
is that pattern's requirements and recommendations with one operation applied
— packages that have no same-named Guix package are removed.  When a group is
added or revised, a package whose Guix name differs while the upstream
software is the same may be mapped instead, recorded per item; a package with
no Guix package at all is removed.  A package is never replaced by a
different one.

A group is kept only when its surviving packages still deliver what its
name promises.  Patterns dropped whole, with the reason:

- `basesystem` — an alias of `base`, by its own summary.
- `bootloader` — grub2, shim and mokutil have no same-named package; the
  bootloader is the installer's job here.
- `minimal_base` — branding, build-key, distribution-release and
  filesystem are distribution artifacts.
- `dhcp_dns_server` — kea has no same-named package, so the survivors
  would deliver DNS only under a name that promises DHCP too.

Every package in a group resolves in Guix proper.  Nonfree software is not
part of this project: an operator who needs it adds their own channels
(`source.channels`) and their own manifest (`software.manifest`).

These files are plain Guix manifests: `guix package -m <file>` installs
the group on any Guix machine, with or without this installer.
